# family_house
:the_family
* Take a house for the family as well, with room for all of them and a hall [once] ?if coven_path() and can_acquire("coven_house")
  $ do acquire("coven_house")
  $ standing += 1
  It is a thing that a family needs, and has rarely been given. It has a long table, and a door that locks from the inside, and a hall that smells of coal smoke and beeswax. You notice that it has been furnished, in advance, by someone who has a very good eye for what people like.
* Put a front over the garage, a taxicab company with a sign and a dozen cabs [once] ?if can_acquire("front_business")
  $ do acquire("front_business")
  $ secrecy += 1
  It is a perfectly honest business. It has twelve cabs and a very good mechanic, and in the loft above the repair shop there is a room that is entered by no stair.
* That is enough for now
  <~
-> :the_family

# tasks
?if coven_size() > 0
  The family are all of them eager, in the odd, unspoken way of a people who have spent thirty five years on a patient errand and are at last allowed to do something with their hands.
  ?if in_circle("prisca") and member("prisca", "status") == "active"
    Prisca wants the river and the railways: who ships what, to whom, and which of them is paid. She says that a city can be read like a ledger, from the bottom, and that this one has been written in a very large hand.
    $ do coven_task("prisca", "watch")
  ?end
  ?if in_circle("fiammetta") and member("fiammetta", "status") == "active"
    Fiammetta wants the door. She says that she will not be surprised by a Chicago, and she has already walked the block twice with a measuring stick and found three ways in that nobody has thought about.
    $ do coven_task("fiammetta", "guard")
  ?end
  ?if in_circle("hanno") and member("hanno", "status") == "active" and _lead != "hanno"
    Hanno wants the Exchange. He has said so with a nod. He has been looking at the ticker in the way that an old dog looks at a new hearth.
    $ do coven_task("hanno", "business")
  ?end
  ?if in_circle("thibault") and member("thibault", "status") == "active" and _lead != "thibault"
    Thibault wants the telephone exchange, and the men who listen at it. He has been learning how it works from a boy of twelve, and is, he says, the first vampire in history to be taught anything by an elevator operator.
    $ do coven_task("thibault", "watch")
  ?end
  ?if in_circle("honorine") and member("honorine", "status") == "active"
    Honorine wants the school, on the South Side, and says that she knows exactly what it is going to need. It is a thing that nobody has asked her, and she has an answer already, in three parts.
  ?end
  ?if in_circle("armand") and member("armand", "status") == "active"
    Armand wants the hospital, a good one, and an hour with every physician in the city who can be induced to talk.
  ?end
  They are your arm in a city where you do not yet have a hand. It is the oldest of your instincts and the most necessary. You tell them that nobody is to act alone. They agree, in the voices of people who have been agreeing to that for a very long time.
?end
<~

# call
## The card
$ do chart("chicago.goldcoast")
$ do discover("the_lake_shore_house")
You have a good evening coat, and a hired car, and an engraved card. It is a long drive on the lake, a mile of lamplit limestone and dark water, with a wind off the lake as cold as a knife. The house, at the end, is of red brick and gray stone, with a lawn and a gate and a driver in a dove gray uniform who stands in the road with the face of a man who has been expecting someone else.
Mrs. Pell receives in a long drawing room at the back, with the curtains drawn and a very good fire, and nine people in it, none of whom breathe. She is small and silver haired and straight, in gray silk, with a string of pearls and an old, plain, penetrating face, and she sits in a high chair without any ceremony.
"Mr. Collins," she says. "Of the sixth line. I had thought the line was a story. I have been wrong about a number of things, and it is always a pleasure to find a new one."
The room has gone very still. You are being weighed, and you can feel the scale.
"There is a custom," she says. "A stranger who comes to Chicago is presented to the house. It is not a law. It is merely very old, and the house is very fond of it. The city is a large one, and a great deal of it is not ours, and there are, I should tell you, three or four other persons who would very much like to know whether you mean to take any of theirs. I find that it is cheaper to ask."
:the_call
* Present yourself in form, with a gift and a courteous speech, and let her put you where she will ?if wealth > 0
  $ b6.call = "form"
  $ standing += 2
  $ cunning_brutal -= 1
  $ do earn(-1)
  She is pleased, and she lets it be seen. The speech is a small one and the gift is large, and the whole of the room relaxes by an exact degree. She tells you, over the course of an hour and a glass of something warm and red, how the city is divided: the Lake Shore for the hospitals and the orchestras, the Yards for the strong arm, the Loop, which is open, for whoever has the nerve, and Mr. Crane, who has none of it and owns a good deal. She does not say that this is a kindness. It is a map, and it is a very good one.
* Go alone, and say that you have come to be looked at, and to look ?if b6.circle != "all"
  $ b6.call = "alone"
  $ cunning_brutal += 1
  $ standing += 1
  She raises one white eyebrow. It is, you gather, a thing that is rarely said to her, and she takes it with the air of a woman who has been offered an unfamiliar dish and finds that she likes it. You look. You are looked at. At the end of an hour you have been told a good deal, in a tone of the lightest courtesy, which you will have to work out for yourself.
* Bring the family into the room, and let her see what the sixth line makes ?if coven_size() > 0 and b6.circle == "all"
  $ b6.call = "family"
  $ cunning_brutal += 1
  $ standing += 3
  $ secrecy -= 1
  $ do discover("the_lake_shore_house")
  It is a large thing to do, and she knows it. They file in behind you, one by one, in their good clothes, and they are, you think, a great deal more than she expected. The stillness of the room changes. One of the nine, a very young man with an expensive face, takes a step back without noticing that he has done so. The sixth line makes its own, and she can see it. She says that the house will be glad of the acquaintance, and she means exactly what she says, and the way in which she means it is not at all comfortable.
  ?if gift_holder("strength") != ""
    {name(gift_holder("strength"))} has, without a word, put one hand on the back of a chair that would, in the ordinary way, have held four men, and it has come, with a small crack, off the floor.
  ?end
  ?if gift_holder("charm") != ""
    {name(gift_holder("charm"))} has made a remark about the pearls, and the old woman, who has not been complimented in thirty years, has colored.
  ?end
* Send regrets, by a clerk, with a note, and a promise to call when the Exchange is in order
  $ b6.call = "regrets"
  $ cunning_brutal += 1
  $ standing -= 2
  $ secrecy += 1
  It is an answer that Chicago understands, and does not like. She does not say what she thinks. She says, in a voice that carries to the end of the room, that Mr. Collins is an admirable man of business, and that the house has every confidence, and that the house will, of course, be watching.
<~

# dawn
## Before the dark ends
$ do discover("the_coin_by_the_yards")
There is a gray at the edge of the sky, and a great flat white light off the lake, and the first of the milk carts, and the strange, constant, enormous sound of Chicago, which never stops, getting up. The house, wherever it is, is asleep. The lamp in the window is lit.
You have been in Chicago for one night. There is an Exchange, and a roof, and a card, and a school, and a man in the Yards who has been asking for you in a gray overcoat, and a song that you heard once before in a cellar in Rome. You think that there is going to be a good deal more.
There is a small, square box of black paper on the step, tied with a gray ribbon. It was not there when you left. Nobody at the house saw who brought it. It is not heavy. Inside, in a nest of cotton, there is a gold coin.
It is very old, and larger than it should be, with a head on one side, and no king's name. It is a woman's head, or a man's, or neither, with a cap of leaves, and the eyes are closed. On the other side there is a single figure, in the shape of six rays, one of them red. Under it there is a card in a plain, quiet, exact hand.
> *The Yards, on Saturday, at the hour of the second shift. I should like to explain what it is for. Do come. A friend.*
:the_coin
* Keep it, and tell nobody, and go
  $ b6.coin = "kept"
  $ cunning_brutal += 1
  It is the oldest of the instincts, and you have it in the bone. A coin is a coin. A friend is a thing that you will decide on Saturday. You put it in your coat, over the heart, where it lies cold and quiet, and does not warm.
* Have it melted down in the Exchange's furnace before breakfast, and send word that the answer is no
  $ b6.coin = "melted"
  $ cunning_brutal += 1
  $ humanity += 1
  It is the oldest of the refusals, and a great pity. A clerk, who does not ask, does it in the cellar, in a crucible, in an hour, and it runs out like a little yellow sun. You do not tell him what it was. You are left with a gleaming bar in your hand, which is nothing at all, and a sound in your head, as of a very large door being shut a long way off.
* Show it to the family, and to Dora, and let them see it, and ask what each of them thinks
  $ b6.coin = "shown"
  $ standing += 1
  $ humanity += 1
  It goes round the table in the early gray, from hand to hand, and nobody says anything for a long time. Dora puts a finger on the head and takes it away. She says that her grandfather had a drawing of that face, in a book, and that he would not say where it came from. It is, you gather, a great deal more than a coin.
$ do rest()
=> next
